ls, weak memory for numbers, makes nasty remarks about colleagues.\n\nCandidate C: anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership, regarded as show-off, not open to new ideas.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as know-it-all, has a hot temper.\n\nWe need to consider criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nLet's assess each:\n\nA: quick correct decisions (good decision quality), calm in crisis (stress), understands tech (competence), but difficulty communicating ideas (crew cooperation, communication), egocentric (cooperation), not willing to further education (adaptability). So strengths but notable concerns.\n\nB: very conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (stress), good at assessing weather (technical), excellent computer skills (technical), weak memory for numbers (attention), makes nasty remarks (crew cooperation, communication). So strong reliability but poor teamwork.\n\nC: anticipates dangerous situations (stress resilience, decision), sees complex connections (technical), excellent spatial vision (technical), very good leadership (crew cooperation), but show-off (cooperation), not open to new ideas (adaptability). So strong technical and decision, but concerns on teamwork and adaptability.\n\nD: responds to unexpected events adequately (stress), concentrates well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical/decision), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility), but hot temper (crew cooperation), know-it-all (cooperation). So strong technical and responsibility, but cooperation issues.\n\nWe need to pick best overall fit. All have some cooperation concerns. Which candidate has least severe? B's nasty remarks is serious. A's egocentric and communication difficulty also serious. C's show-off and not open to new ideas also problematic. D's hot temper and know-it-all also problematic. Which is least? Possibly D: hot temper could be serious, but maybe manageable? D also takes responsibility seriously, good problem solving, concentration. C's not open to new ideas could affect adaptability. B's nasty remarks is interpersonal. A's communication difficulty may affect crew. I'd lean D as best overall, despite hot temper, but we need to argue.\n\nWe can start by presenting recommendation for D, citing strengths and acknowledging concerns, and propose to discuss others. Provide evidence.\n\nWe must include METADATA_JSON with vote.\n\nLet's produce."}
